{ [%csl Ge4][%cal Ge2e4] }
1. e4 { [%csl Ge4,Ge6][%cal Ge7e6] } 1... e6 { [%csl Ge6,Ge4,Gd4][%cal Gd2d4] } 2. d4 { [%csl Ge6,Ge4,Gd4,Gd5][%cal Gd7d5] } 2... d5 { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gd4,Ge4,Gd2][%cal Gb1d2] } 3. Nd2 { : The opening position of the Tarrasch Variation of the French Defense.
The Tarrasch Variation is an extremely popular response to the French Defense. It is a strong alternative to the Paulsen Variation, 3.Nc3, that has dual purpose. It allows white to both avoid the uncomfortable Winnower pin, 3...Bb4, and support the d4 pawn with c3, forming a strong pawn chain. In return for these advantages, white temporarily obstructs its queen and dark-squared bishop, and gives up on aggressive knight maneuvers resulting from Nc3. There are several responses black could play: } { [%csl Ge6,Gd5,Gd4,Ge4,Gd2] } 3... Nf6 { Closed Variation. Black's most common response. While slightly weaker theoretically compared to 3...c4, practically this gives black stronger chances at a win. This response puts more pressure on the e4 pawn, forcing it to advance to e5, slightly overextending. This study won't cover this variation. See Coldfield1994's other studies for analysis of the Closed Variation. } (3... c5 { Open System. Black's 2nd most common response, seen more at high-level. This response is black's strongest, as it instantly brings black close to equality at the cost of being very drawish. Black simply puts more pressure on the d4 pawn since the queen's defense of it is obstructed by the knight. This study will follow this response. }) (3... Be7 { Morozevich Variation. Uncommon response, seen more at high level. Usually considered a waiting move, this simply develops the bishop in preparation to castle. Black can then develop advantageously in response to white's choice in development, at the cost of some tempo. This can throw white out of theory, and is therefore seen more at high level. This study won't cover this variation. See Coldfield1994's other studies for analysis of the Morozevich Variation. }) (3... dxe4 { Rubenstein Variation transposition. Black's 3rd most common response. At the cost of some developmental advantage, black can simplify the position and avoid theory by exchanging pawns. After recapture with 4.Nxe4, black argues that its developmental disadvantage is balanced by white's misplaced/overextended knight. However, at high-level this practically is weaker than 3...c5 with fewer draws and wins. This study won't cover this transposition. See Coldfield1994's other studies for analysis of the Rubenstein Variation. }) (3... Nc6? { Guimard Defense. Uncommon response, seen more at low-level. Mistake. This obstructs black's c-pawn from advancing to c5, giving up some possible pressure on d4. This allows white to more easily stabilize its structure and set up its attack. Due to the variation's rarity, black sees higher wins at high level but lower draws/wins, and is best played when a win is needed with black. This study won't cover this variation. See Coldfield1994's other studies for analysis of the Guimard Defense. }) (3... a6 { Modern System. Uncommon response. One of black's stronger theoretical responses, at high-level it practically sees more wins at the cost of slightly lower wins/draws, through this may be due to its relative rarity. The response defends b5, preventing white from any bishop checks before beginning to attack white's center while white is still developing its structure. This study won't cover this variation. See Coldfield1994's other studies for analysis of the Modern System. }) (3... f5? { Haberditz Variation. Uncommon response. Mistake. Black attempts to pressure e4, but white can take either pawn or push to e5 and gain an advantage, as black now has an overextended pawn and weak king with an exchange, or cannot easily attack white's pawn chain with e5. }) (3... c6? { Uncommon response. Mistake. Black attempts to reinforce its pawns creating a pawn triangle; however, this gives white tempo to develop its structure. }) (3... h6? { Uncommon response. Mistake. Black attempts to defend g5 from any future bishop or knight attacks; however, this gives white tempo to develop its structure. }) *


