{ The Open System is black's 2nd most common response, seen more at high-level. This response is black's strongest, as it instantly brings black close to equality at the cost of being very drawish. Black simply puts more pressure on the d4 pawn since the queen's defense of it is obstructed by the knight. }
4. exd5 { White's most common response. This immediately breaks open the center, forcing black to either overextend its central pawns with exd5, or recapture with the queen, which can be targeted for tempo development. This also often sacrifices the d5 pawn temporarily. } (4. Ngf3!! { Euwe-Keres Line. White's strongest, 2nd most common response. White develops its knight, defending the d4 pawn and preparing to castle king's side. }) (4. c3?! { Suchting Line. White's 3rd most common response, seen more at low-level. Inaccuracy. Black can trade down the center without any real positional disadvantage, leaving white with an isolated queen's pawn that is difficult to defend and a weaker king. }) (4. dxc5 { White's 4th most common response. This trades off the central pawn, opening up white's d-file and allowing white to develop more aggressively. Many prefer to wait until black's dark-squared bishop moves once to trade, as this gains white a tempo after recapture. However, white can use the early opening of the center just as advantageously in this position. }) (4. Bb5+ { Uncommon response. White can check black's king, forcing black to trade light-squared bishops. This increases the likelihood of a draw as there are fewer pieces on the board, and lets black easily trade off its weak French bishop. }) *


