{ White develops its knight, defending the d4 pawn and preparing to castle king's side. }
4... cxd4! { Black's strongest, most common response, seen more at high-level. Black begins a pawn trade, removing one of white's central pawns, beginning to simplify the position. } (4... Nc6?! { Black's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy. This simply develops the knight, putting another attacker on white's d4 pawn, threatening to take. However, if black tries to now simplify the position, black ends up with an isolated queen's pawn, which is difficult to defend. }) (4... Nf6 { Black's 3rd most common response, seen more at high-level. This develops the knight, putting a second attacker on e4. It promotes white to play e5, pushing away black's knight to d7. }) (4... dxe4?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This trades central pawns like the Rubenstein Variation. However, unlike the Rubenstein, when white recaptures with Nxd4, white's d4 pawn is defended twice, allowing white to offer a queen trade when black captures with cxd4. Accepting or declining the trade leaves white with a developmental advantage. }) (4... a6?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. This defends the b5 square, preventing any bishop checks or pins. However, this gives white tempo to continue development in in the center with Bd3 or start to break open the center with dxc5. }) *


