{ Black begins a pawn trade, removing one of white's central pawns, beginning to simplify the position. }
5. Nxd4 { White's most common response. White immediately recaptures with its knight, centralizing it. } (5. exd5 { White's 2nd most common response, seen more at high-level. See Chapter X for transposition. }) (5. Bb5+?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. White checks black's king, forcing black to intercept. Black's best option is to offer a bishop trade, which white takes. This trade removes black's bad French bishop, and is a slight mistake for white. }) 5... Nc6 { Black's most common response. This develops the queen's side knight, both offering a knight trade and threatening to take the d4 knight. } (5... Nf6 { Black's 2nd most common, strong response. This develops the king's side knight, adding a second attacker to e4, pressuring it to trade with exd5, which can now be recaptured with the knight, preserving black's pawn chain. }) (5... dxe4? { Black's 3rd most common response. Mistake. Black trades its other pawn, opening up the center further. However, this develops white's other knight to the center with Nxe4, simultaneously defending the d4 knight with the queen. Black cannot continue to attack the d4 knight with e5, as after Nb5 and a queen trade that loses white's castling rights, black cannot defend against Nc7, forking the rook and king without sacrificing its dark-squared bishop. }) (5... e5?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. This pressures the d4 knight, forcing it to move. However, this breaks black's pawn chain, undefending the d5 pawn. White can also counter-attack e5 by simply retreating with N4f3. }) (5... Bc5?? { Uncommon response. Slight blunder. Black develops its bishop, attacking the d4 knight. However, white can counter-attack with N2b3, threatening the dark-squared bishop, forcing it to retreat or trade, losing the bishop pair. This counter-attack also helps develop the d2 knight. }) (5... Ne7 { Uncommon, strong response. This develops the king's side knight, defending the d5 pawn again and preserving black's pawn chain in a trade. This also prevents white from later pinning the knight to the queen, enables a Nf5 jump in the future, and defends the c6 square for when black develops its other knight. However, this temporarily obstructs the queen and dark-squared bishop. }) *


