{ Black trades pawns and develops the queen's side knight, both offering a knight trade and threatening to take the d4 knight. }
6. Bb5! { White's strongest, most common response. This develops the light-squared bishop, pinning black's knight to the king and preparing white to castle king's side. It also threatens Nxc6, which would end with a king-rook fork if undefended. } (6. Nxc6 { White's 2nd most common response. This simply trades knights, reducing the tension in the center, allowing white to develop normally by defending the e4 pawn and attacking the center with c4. }) (6. c3?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. White defends its d4 knight, opening the queen's diagonal. However, black can still trade knights, as this will trade everything in the center, leaving white's queen slightly overextended and its king weak. }) 6... Bd7! { Black's strongest, most common response. This defends the c6 knight, preventing a king-rook fork after trades. If white trades knights, it also forces white to retreat its bishop. Lastly, it also vacates the c8 square for the rook or queen in the future. } (6... Qb6? { Uncommon response. Mistake. Black develops its queen aggressively, defending the c6 knight and threatening to take the d4 knight. However, white can now trade central pawns with exd5, forcing black to recapture with the pawn, leaving it with an isolated pawn. White can not take the knight without hanging its own knight and leaving white with a passed pawn. }) (6... Ne7 { Uncommmon response. This defends the c6 knight, preventing a king-rook fork after trades. If white trades knights, it also forces white to retreat its bishop. However, this does obstruct the normal development of the dark-squared bishop and can tangle up black's pieces while white can reposition more easily. }) *


