{ This simply develops the knight, putting another attacker on white's d4 pawn, threatening to take. However, if black tries to now simplify the position, black ends up with an isolated queen's pawn, which is difficult to defend. }
5. exd5! { White's strongest, most common response. This trades pawns in the center, damaging black's king's side pawn chain or overextending its queen, which can be attacked for tempo development. } (5. Bb5?! { White's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy. White attempts to pin black's knight to the king with its light-squared bishop. However, black can simply push the bishop away with a6 or force white to trade its bishop for the knight. This damages black's pawn structure, but opens up the b-file and wins black the bishop pair. }) (5. c3?! { White's 3rd most common response. Inaccuracy. This defends the d4 pawn while opening up the queen's diagonal. However, this allows black to trade away the central pawns, weakening white's king's safety. }) (5. dxc5?! { Uncommon response. Inaccuracy. White trades central pawns, opening up the center. However, this allows black to develop its bishop advantageously during recapture. Trading pawns should instead be done after black moves its bishop once, gaining a free tempo for white. }) 5... exd5! { Black's strongest, most common response. This simply recaptures the pawn, opening up black's king somewhat, but avoiding tempo attacks that would occur with Qxd5. } (5... Qxd5?! { Black's 2nd most common response. Inaccuracy. This recaptures the pawn with the queen, maintaining black's king's side pawn structure, leaving the king more secure. However, this allows white to gain tempo development by attacking the slightly overextended queen. }) (5... Nxd4?? { Uncommon response. Blunder. Black decides not to recapture the pawn, and instead takes white's other central pawn on d4. However, white can simply trade knights and then later take another of black's pawns, leaving black with an overextended central pawn in addition. }) *


